[id="ClickHouse",reftext="ClickHouse"]
=== ClickHouse

Use `symadmin module install clickhouse` to install driver files, or copy your own files into the `lib` sub-directory.

ClickHouse is a column-oriented database for analytics. SymmetricDS supports ClickHouse as a write-only target, where
changes from another database are loaded into ClickHouse tables. Capturing changes from ClickHouse and using it as a
source are not supported, and neither is a bulk loader.

==== Setup

ClickHouse runs as a write-only node in SymmetricDS.  See <<Write-Only>> for details on setting up a write-only node in SymmetricDS.

ifndef::pro[]

.Example properties to setup a ClickHouse write-only node
----
load.only=true
target.db.driver=com.clickhouse.jdbc.ClickHouseDriver
target.db.url=jdbc:clickhouse://<host>:8123/<database_name>
target.db.user=<clickhouse_user>
target.db.password=<clickhouse_password>
----

endif::pro[]

Next, set the `data_loader_type` of each <<Channels, channel>> that carries data for ClickHouse tables to *clickhouse*, which includes
the `reload` channel because it is set to *bulk* by default. The ClickHouse data loader is what makes updates and deletes work and
what clears a table before a reload. A channel left on another data loader type does not use it, so updates and deletes are
reported as conflicts and a reload fails when it clears the table.

The `clickhouse` and `ch` JDBC sub-protocols are both recognized. The driver connects over HTTP, which is port 8123
on a server that is not using TLS. The database named in the URL is the default schema for the target tables.
The user needs permission to create, alter, and drop tables in that database, and to insert, update, and delete rows.

==== How Tables Are Created

When a table does not exist in ClickHouse, SymmetricDS creates it from the source definition as a `MergeTree` table:

[source,SQL]
----
CREATE TABLE "orders"(
    "id" Int32,
    "region" String,
    "total" Nullable(Decimal(10, 2))
)
ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY ("id", "region") SETTINGS enable_block_number_column = 1, enable_block_offset_column = 1;
----

* The primary key of the source becomes the `ORDER BY` sorting key. A table without a primary key uses `ORDER BY tuple()`.
* Columns that are not required are created as `Nullable(...)`. Key columns are never nullable, because ClickHouse
rejects a nullable sorting key.
* The two table settings are needed for `UPDATE` statements. Without them, an update fails with error 48.
* Literal default values (numbers, text, booleans, dates, and timestamps) are created as a `DEFAULT` on the column. A default that is an
expression, such as `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` or a sequence, is not created, because it would not mean the same thing in ClickHouse.
A column added with a default has that value in the rows that were already in the table.
* An auto-increment column is created with `DEFAULT generateSerialID('table_column')`. ClickHouse only runs the default when a row is
inserted without a value for the column, which SymmetricDS never does, so the values come from the source. The default itself requires
ClickHouse Keeper to be configured on the server. Without it the table is still created and loaded, but an insert that leaves the column out fails.
* Indexes, foreign keys, and triggers are not created. Indexes are not supported in this release, and ClickHouse does not have foreign keys or triggers.

The following table shows how types are mapped.

[cols="40,60", options="header"]
|===
|Source Type |ClickHouse Type

|BIT, BOOLEAN |Bool
|TINYINT |Int8
|SMALLINT |Int16
|INTEGER |Int32
|BIGINT |Int64
|REAL |Float32
|FLOAT, DOUBLE |Float64
|DECIMAL, NUMERIC |Decimal(precision, scale). ClickHouse allows a precision of at most 76. When the source has no precision, or more than 76, the precision is 38 and the scale is at most 10.
|CHAR, VARCHAR, LONGVARCHAR, CLOB |String
|BINARY, VARBINARY, BLOB |String, with the column comment `symds:binary`
|DATE |Date32
|TIMESTAMP |DateTime64(6)
|TIME |String
|===

Binary data is stored as bytes in a `String` column. The column comment `symds:binary` records that the column holds binary
data, so the loader decodes the hex or base64 from the data stream into bytes. Do not remove the comment, or the encoded text is
stored instead. Use `hex()` or `base64Encode()` to display such a column. A time of day is stored as text, because ClickHouse
has no type for it.

A table that already exists in ClickHouse can use any of its types as the target. A `UUID` column accepts the text form of a
UUID, so a source column of 36 characters loads into it directly.

==== Schema Changes

When the definition of a source table changes, SymmetricDS alters the ClickHouse table:

* A new column is added with `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN`.
* A removed column is dropped with `ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN`. ClickHouse does not allow dropping a column that is part of the sorting key.
* A change of type, size, or whether a column is required is applied with `ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY COLUMN`.
* A change of a column between text and binary is not detected, because both are stored as a `String`.
* A change to the primary key is not applied, and a warning is logged. ClickHouse cannot change the sorting key of an existing table. Create
a new table and copy the data when the key must change.

An existing table is never dropped and rebuilt to apply a change.

==== Updates, Deletes, and Conflicts

ClickHouse applies `UPDATE` and `DELETE` statements, but it always reports that zero rows were changed, whether or not a row matched.
The default data loader treats a count of zero as a conflict, which would send every update and delete into conflict resolution.
For that reason, the ClickHouse writer treats every `UPDATE` and `DELETE` as successful and counts it as one row.

This has the following effects:

* Conflict detection does not work on a ClickHouse target. An update or delete of a row that does not exist is silently ignored,
and the loader never falls back to an insert. Conflict detection and resolution settings do not apply to updates and deletes.
* Inserts are counted correctly. In the batch statistics, the insert count is the number of rows inserted. The update and delete
counts are the number of statements sent, not the number of rows changed.
* ClickHouse does not enforce keys. Loading the same insert twice, for example when a batch is sent again after a failure, stores
the row twice. An insert never conflicts with an existing row.
* Rows are found by primary key only. The loader does not add the old values of other columns to the `WHERE` clause, because a
row that does not match would be skipped without any sign of it.
* Updating the primary key is not supported. ClickHouse cannot update a column of the sorting key (error 420, `CANNOT_UPDATE_COLUMN`),
so an update that changes a primary key value fails the batch with the error "ClickHouse does not support updating the primary key",
which names the table and the key columns. The batch stays in error until the change is removed or the target is corrected by hand.
* ClickHouse has no multi-statement transactions. A batch that fails part way through can leave the rows of its earlier statements loaded, and the retry then inserts those rows again.
* Updates and deletes are heavier operations in ClickHouse than inserts, which are fastest when sent in large batches. A source that
mostly sends inserts suits ClickHouse best.

==== Initial Load and Reload

The initial load creates missing tables and loads rows with insert statements. A reload clears the target table first with the statement from
the `initial.load.delete.first.sql` parameter, which is `delete from %s` by default. ClickHouse does not accept a `DELETE` without a `WHERE`
clause, so SymmetricDS runs `truncate table` instead. A custom statement that has a `WHERE` clause is run as it is.

==== Limitations

* Capturing changes and using ClickHouse as a source are not supported.
* There is no bulk loader. Batches are loaded with JDBC batch inserts.
* ClickHouse Cloud, including TLS connections on port 8443, has not been validated.
* The primary key of an existing table cannot be changed.
* Table names that contain the source node ID, which are used when the target external ID is in use, are not supported.
